# Fixed defect 2439, 2026-11-10, lane b30-pkg: `lib/internal.hero`, a file
# named `internal` below the root, named `use lib/internal` (panel 244's
# critic).
#
# SYMPTOM: `private_module`, *a module under an `internal` folder is named
# only by the module that folder belongs to*, though the file stands under
# no `internal` folder: the compiler applies Go's element rule, a part
# `internal` after the first, and the message stated another.
#
# FIX: the message states the rule applied, a part `internal` after the
# first, a folder's name or the module's own, the path before the last such
# part owning it; the rule itself is unchanged (panel 244 rules on how the
# specification states it). So `internal.hero` at the root, a first part,
# builds, and `a/internal/b/internal/c` is `a/internal/b`'s.
use a/internal/b/internal/c  #~ private_module
use internal
use lib/internal as li  #~ private_module

function main()
    print(internal.two() + li.two() + c.two())
